Implanon Insertion / Removal
General Practice Other
Request for subdermal contraception, Medial upper arm location, Localized anaesthetic use, Negative pregnancy test

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-018
Reason for Visit: Acute localized infection and severe pain surrounding an expired subdermal contraceptive implant, requiring immediate extraction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient with an expired Implanon rod presenting with sudden, rapidly spreading erythema, swelling, and severe localized pain in the medial upper arm over the past 24 hours. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to rule out advancing systemic cellulitis, perform an immediate surgical extraction of the infected foreign body under local anesthesia, and initiate empiric antibiotics. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period posed a high risk of deep fascial infection and systemic sepsis.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "My old arm implant has suddenly become hot, red, and incredibly painful, and I can't straighten my arm."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a request for removal of a long-acting reversible etonogestrel contraceptive due to expiry and an acute complication. The implant was due for removal 6 months ago. Over the past 24 hours, the patient developed a severe, throbbing pain over the palpable sub-dermal rod in the medial aspect of the non-dominant upper arm. The pain is exacerbated by any movement of the biceps. There is a complete absence of absolute contraindications such as current breast cancer, though the immediate concern is the localized infection. The patient is highly distressed by the visible redness and swelling.
Functional Status: Independent, but completely guarding the left arm against the chest due to severe localized pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; fear of the infection spreading to the bloodstream.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 105 | Temp: 37.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, anxious, supporting the left arm. Low-grade fever present.
Physical Exam: Integumentary: Examination of the left medial upper arm reveals a 4cm area of intense erythema, induration, and warmth directly overlying the palpable subdermal rod. Exquisite tenderness is noted upon light palpation. No purulent fluctuance is currently visible, but the implant acts as an infected nidus. The procedure requires the administration of local anaesthetic (lidocaine) prior to the small stab incision allowing extraction via forceps. Expected investigation results include a negative point-of-care urine pregnancy test ensuring non-pregnant status prior to any subsequent contraceptive planning, though the immediate priority is source control of the infection.
Environmental Observations: Safe, clean environment with adequate lighting for a minor surgical procedure.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Implanon Removal (Complicated by Acute Localized Cellulitis) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis (Upper Extremity) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The erythema and pain are strictly localized to the subcutaneous tissue directly overlying the implant, without generalized arm swelling or venous engorgement.

Necrotizing Fasciitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of hemorrhagic bullae, crepitus, or profound systemic shock, though early intervention is required to prevent progression.

Simple Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of fever, deep induration, and severe throbbing pain, which indicate a true bacterial infection rather than a surface allergy.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient developed an acute, rapidly progressive localized cellulitis centered around a retained, expired subdermal contraceptive implant. Telephone triage correctly recognized the implant as a contaminated foreign body nidus requiring immediate source control. Urgent face-to-face assessment was mandated to perform an immediate surgical extraction to remove the source of the infection, irrigate the site, and commence systemic antibiotics. Deferring this intervention until the next in-hours period would have allowed the localized infection to spread rapidly through the fascial planes of the upper arm, inevitably leading to systemic sepsis and emergency surgical admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered 1% Lidocaine with Epinephrine subcutaneously. Performed a small stab incision and successfully extracted the intact etonogestrel rod via mosquito forceps. Minimal localized bleeding was controlled with steri-strips and a pressure bandage. Copiously irrigated the pocket with sterile saline. Prescribed oral Flucloxacillin 500mg QID for 7 days. Conducted a discussion of expected bleeding pattern changes and the expected return to fertility following removal.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the transition off hormonal contraception, commit to immediately placing a box of condoms physically on top of your pillow every morning when you make the bed. This unavoidable visual and physical cue acts as an immediate prompt to use barrier protection, successfully bridging the gap between the implant removal and establishing a new long-term contraceptive plan.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 48 hours to assess the resolution of the cellulitis and discuss future contraceptive options.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if the redness rapidly spreads beyond the pen mark drawn on your arm tonight, or if you develop uncontrollable shivering or confusion.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Expect post-procedural bruising and mild localized tenderness. The infection should begin to visibly recede within 48 hours of starting the antibiotics. You are now immediately fertile.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek urgent medical review tomorrow if the wound begins to discharge foul-smelling yellow pus or if your fever spikes above 38.5°C.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The expired implant became a focal point for a bacterial skin infection. We had to remove it immediately to stop the infection from spreading, and the antibiotics will clear the remaining bacteria in the skin.
Actionable Steps: Keep the pressure bandage completely dry for 48 hours. Take the antibiotics strictly on an empty stomach. Use barrier contraception immediately.

